steps.body:
`switch` evaluates a value once, then compares it with each `case` from top to bottom. The first matching case runs its lines. `break` leaves the switch. `default` runs when no case matches, like a final `else`.

```javascript
switch (size) {
  case "S":
    console.log("Small");
    break;
  case "L":
    console.log("Large");
    break;
  default:
    console.log("Medium");
}
```

Case values can be numbers or strings. The comparison is strict equality (`===`), so the type must match too: the string `"2"` never matches `case 2`. Convert first with `Number(text)` if your value is text.

A common pattern is to declare a `let` before the switch and assign it in each case, then print once after it. The last clause does not need a `break` because nothing comes after it.

example.code:
const day = 3;
let name;

switch (day) {
  case 1:
    name = "Monday";
    break;
  case 2:
    name = "Tuesday";
    break;
  case 3:
    name = "Wednesday";
    break;
  case 4:
    name = "Thursday";
    break;
  case 5:
    name = "Friday";
    break;
  case 6:
    name = "Saturday";
    break;
  case 7:
    name = "Sunday";
    break;
  default:
    name = "Unknown day";
}

console.log(`Day ${day} is ${name}`);

