steps.body:
Once a case matches, JavaScript runs every line below it until it hits a `break` or the end of the switch. It does not stop at the next `case` label. That is called fall-through.

You use it on purpose by stacking empty cases, so several values share one body:

```javascript
switch (key) {
  case "y":
  case "Y":
    console.log("Yes");
    break;
  default:
    console.log("No");
}
```

Both `"y"` and `"Y"` fall into the same `console.log`. Stacked labels are the only place fall-through is normal.

The flip side is a classic bug: forget a `break` in a case that has code, and the next case's code runs too. When a switch prints extra lines, look for a missing `break` first. If you ever fall through on purpose after real code, leave a comment saying so.

example.code:
const day = "sun";

switch (day) {
  case "sat":
  case "sun":
    console.log("Weekend");
    break;
  case "mon":
  case "tue":
  case "wed":
  case "thu":
  case "fri":
    console.log("Weekday");
    break;
  default:
    console.log("Not a day");
}

// A missing break on purpose
const level = 1;

switch (level) {
  case 1:
    console.log("Level 1 unlocked");
  // no break, so execution falls into case 2
  case 2:
    console.log("Level 2 unlocked");
    break;
  default:
    console.log("No levels unlocked");
}

