steps.body:
A switch is a natural fit for picking an operation by name. Declare the result with `let` before the switch so every case can assign it and the code after the switch can use it.

All cases share one block scope, the braces of the switch. So two cases cannot both declare `const total`: that is a SyntaxError for a duplicate name. When a case needs its own `const` or `let`, wrap its body in braces:

```javascript
case "avg": {
  const total = a + b;
  result = total / 2;
  break;
}
```

Now `total` exists only inside that case. Put `break` inside the braces, at the end of the case.

If `default` handles a bad operator by printing a message, `result` stays `undefined`, so check `result !== undefined` before printing the answer.

example.code:
const a = 12;
const b = 4;
const op = "avg";
let result;

switch (op) {
  case "+":
    result = a + b;
    break;
  case "-":
    result = a - b;
    break;
  case "*":
    result = a * b;
    break;
  case "/":
    result = a / b;
    break;
  case "avg": {
    const total = a + b;
    result = total / 2;
    break;
  }
  default:
    console.log("Unknown operator");
}

if (result !== undefined) {
  console.log(`${a} ${op} ${b} = ${result}`);
}

